# vergeio_auth_source (Resource)

Creates an external identity provider so SSO configuration is reviewed like any other change. `driver` is one of `azure`, `google`, `gitlab`, `okta`, `openid`, `openid-well-known`, `oauth2`, or `verge.io`. Changing `driver` replaces the auth source. VergeOS does not change the driver of an existing source.

`settings` is one JSON object, usually written with `jsonencode`. Do not put `client_secret` in that object. Set `client_secret_wo` and `client_secret_wo_version` instead. Terraform stores the version, not the secret. Increment the version to send a new secret. Changing `client_secret_wo` without changing the version does not update the auth source. `client_secret_wo` requires Terraform 1.11 or OpenTofu 1.11.

VergeOS replaces the settings document on update. A partial document would delete `client_secret` and every key it did not mention. This resource sends the configured keys, and the client merges them with the stored document first, so omitted keys are kept, including `client_secret` when the version did not change. A key you remove from `settings` therefore stays on the auth source and Terraform stops tracking it. Replace the auth source to drop a settings key. The server-managed `debug` key inside the stored document is not tracked. The `debug` argument is the separate column.

VergeOS rejects delete while users or OIDC applications still reference the source.

`vergeio_user.auth_source` is the key of this resource. Use `tonumber(vergeio_auth_source.example.id)` when a user should authenticate with it.

